        "problem": "If $T=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ -12 & 6\\end{aligned}]$, then ${T}^{6}=$",
        "worked_solution": "Computing ${T}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{2} & =T\\cdot T \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ -12 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ -12 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${T}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $T$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${T}^{6}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{6} & ={T}^{2}\\cdot {T}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {T}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{T}^{6}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
